  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>Rasbora daniconius usually grows to 6-8 cm in length; the largest specimen ever measured supposedly reached 15 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>Imported from India. The species is found in Bengal together with such well-known and popular ornamental fishes as zebra danio (Danio rerio), rosy barb (Pethia conchonius), dwarf gourami (Colisa lalius) and chameleon fish (Badis badis).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>The water temperature can be between 18 and 28°C. Any tap water suitable as drinking water is suitable for care.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>Omnivores; plants are ignored.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>The animals are completely peaceful. Like the majority of minnows, this species appreciates the company of its conspecifics and should be kept in a group of at least five, preferably 10-15 specimens. It is a good contrast fish to the above species in the community aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/fisharchive/rasbora-daniconius-6/",
      "html": "<p>R. daniconius are free spawners without brood care.</p>"
    }
  ],
